# elliptic 6.6.1 — CVE-2025-14505 (LOW), recorded 2026-08-10
#
# https://access.redhat.com/security/cve/CVE-2025-14505
#
# ECDSA signing produces a truncated `k` when an interim value has leading
# zeros, which is cryptanalysable. There is no fixed version: 6.6.1 is the
# latest release upstream has published, so there is nothing to bump to.
#
# Nothing here signs anything with it. It arrives through
# `@backstage/cli-module-build` -> `node-stdlib-browser` -> `crypto-browserify`
# -> `browserify-sign`, the polyfill set the Backstage CLI keeps on hand to shim
# Node's `crypto` when a browser bundle imports it. This package is a build-time
# dependency of the CLI, it is absent from every published `dist/`, and the
# plugin's browser code reaches for Web Crypto rather than a polyfill. Drop this
# entry as soon as upstream ships a patched `elliptic`.
CVE-2025-14505

# @octokit/request 6.2.8, @octokit/request-error 3.0.3,
# @octokit/plugin-paginate-rest 6.1.2 — CVE-2025-25290, CVE-2025-25289, CVE-2025-25288
#
# Pulled in transitively by @backstage/core-compat-api -> @backstage/integration-react
# -> @backstage/integration, which the `/alpha` entry point needs for `compatWrapper`.
# @backstage/integration@2.0.3 is the latest release and still pins `@octokit/rest@^19`
# and `@octokit/auth-app@^4`, so there is no upstream version to move to — every
# Backstage app carries these today. Forcing the patched octokit lines (8.x / 9.x)
# onto v4-era `@octokit/core` crosses two majors, including octokit's move to native
# fetch, and would break @backstage/integration's GitHub credential path.
#
# That path is never reached by this plugin: it talks to GitHub through its own
# GraphQL client, and @backstage/integration arrives only as a transitive of the
# compatibility layer. Drop these once Backstage ships an integration release on
# a patched octokit.
CVE-2025-25288
CVE-2025-25289
CVE-2025-25290

# uuid 3.4.0 — CVE-2026-41907
#
# Reached only through @material-table/core@3.2.5, a transitive dependency of
# @backstage/core-components (which pins ^3.1.0). That package calls
# `require("uuid").default.v4()`, and uuid removed its default export in 7.0.0,
# so none of the patched releases (11.x / 12.x / 13.x) can be substituted without
# breaking it at runtime.
#
# The only component that pulls it in is core-components' `Table`, which this
# plugin does not render, and `resolutions` in a library package would not reach
# consumers anyway. Drop this entry once @backstage/core-components moves to a
# @material-table/core release that uses a named uuid import.
CVE-2026-41907
